\name{BaseRBxpLayerProcessor}
\alias{BaseRBxpLayerProcessor}
\title{Base R bxp() Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads a \code{graphics::bxp()} call as the box plot it draws.

\code{bxp()} is the drawing half of \code{boxplot()}: \code{boxplot.default()} computes the
five-number summaries and then hands them to \code{bxp()}, which puts the boxes,
whiskers, medians and outliers on the page. Calling it directly is how a
caller draws boxes from summaries they already have -- from
\code{boxplot(plot = FALSE)}, from \code{boxplot.stats()}, or computed elsewhere
entirely -- and it is one of the twelve calls #262 found drawing while the
save reported no plot at all.
\subsection{What is the same, and what is not}{

The marks are identical. Drawn off-screen and echoed through
\code{gridGraphics}, \code{bxp(z)} and the \code{boxplot()} call that produced \code{z} emit
the same grob names in the same order -- \code{polygon-1}, \code{segments-1},
\code{points-1}, ... -- because the same code drew them. Every selector
\code{BaseRBoxplotLayerProcessor} builds, including the index shift each box
with no outliers puts on the boxes after it, therefore addresses a \code{bxp()}
chart unchanged, and this class inherits all of it.

The one difference is where the summaries come from. \code{boxplot()} is handed
observations, so its processor replays \code{boxplot(plot = FALSE)} to recover
them; \code{bxp()} is handed the summaries themselves, in its first argument.
Replaying \code{boxplot()} on \emph{that} would read the six-element list as six
groups of numbers and summarise them -- so the only thing this class
overrides is \code{read_stats()}.

Two things \code{bxp()} shares with \code{boxplot()} are shared including their
limits: \code{horizontal = TRUE} means the same thing to both, and \verb{at =}
repositions boxes without reordering the drawing in either, so a
non-monotonic \code{at} reads in drawing order here exactly as it already does
for \code{boxplot()}.
}
